{"reason": "The initial plan's core direction is to address the observed overfitting and high variance by switching from pseudo-labeling retraining to a 5-fold cross-validation ensemble, and by reducing the learning rate and increasing warmup. Historical experiments show that pseudo-labeling with retraining (as in the current code) actually hurt performance (validation log loss worsened from 0.3882 to 0.4154), confirming the plan's direction. The successful historical experience of a 5-fold ensemble (used in a similar experiment) supports this change. The failed experiment with mixup and pseudo-labeling is avoided. Therefore, the modifications are concentrated in the 'training_evaluation' module to implement the cross-validation ensemble and adjust hyperparameters. 'model_design' and 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' are kept unchanged as they are not the source of the issues and remain effective.", "module": ["training_evaluation"], "plan": {"training_evaluation": "Replace the current single-split (train_idx/val_idx) training with a 5-fold stratified cross-validation ensemble. For each fold, train a separate ModernBERT-large model on 4/5 of the training data and validate on the held-out 1/5. Use 5-fold stratified splits based on 'author' labels. For each fold, save the best model checkpoint. After training all 5 folds, generate out-of-fold (OOF) predictions for validation (by averaging fold predictions where each sample was in validation) and average the predictions from all 5 folds for the test set. Additionally, reduce the learning rate from 3e-5 to 2e-5 and increase warmup ratio from 0.1 to 0.15 to mitigate overfitting observed in the initial run (train loss dropped to 0.028 while validation log loss jumped to 0.5216). Remove the pseudo-labeling retraining block entirely. Maintain the same data loaders and tokenization; the main loop iterates over folds. Expected result: validation log loss improves from ~0.388 to ~0.35-0.37, and test predictions are more robust and less overfitted."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan's core direction is to address the observed overfitting and high variance by switching from pseudo-labeling retraining to a 5-fold cross-validation ensemble, and by reducing the learning rate and increasing warmup. Historical experiments show that pseudo-labeling with retraining (as in the current code) actually hurt performance (validation log loss worsened from 0.3882 to 0.4154), confirming the plan's direction. The successful historical experience of a 5-fold ensemble (used in a similar experiment) supports this change. The failed experiment with mixup and pseudo-labeling is avoided. Therefore, the modifications are concentrated in the 'training_evaluation' module to implement the cross-validation ensemble and adjust hyperparameters. 'model_design' and 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' are kept unchanged as they are not the source of the issues and remain effective.",
  "module": ["training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "training_evaluation": "Replace the current single-split (train_idx/val_idx) training with a 5-fold stratified cross-validation ensemble. For each fold, train a separate ModernBERT-large model on 4/5 of the training data and validate on the held-out 1/5. Use 5-fold stratified splits based on 'author' labels. For each fold, save the best model checkpoint. After training all 5 folds, generate out-of-fold (OOF) predictions for validation (by averaging fold predictions where each sample was in validation) and average the predictions from all 5 folds for the test set. Additionally, reduce the learning rate from 3e-5 to 2e-5 and increase warmup ratio from 0.1 to 0.15 to mitigate overfitting observed in the initial run (train loss dropped to 0.028 while validation log loss jumped to 0.5216). Remove the pseudo-labeling retraining block entirely. Maintain the same data loaders and tokenization; the main loop iterates over folds. Expected result: validation log loss improves from ~0.388 to ~0.35-0.37, and test predictions are more robust and less overfitted."
