e-5. Use linear warmup for 10% of training steps followed by cosine decay to 0. Keep AdamW with weight_decay=0.01 and max_norm=1.0 gradient clipping. Monitor validation log loss (not validation loss) and implement early stopping with patience 3 epochs, saving the best model based on validation log loss. Generate final test predictions directly from the fine-tuned ModernBERT, ensuring probabilities are clipped and normalized per the competition requirements."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution shows severe overfitting: ModernBERT training loss drops from 0.4684 to 0.0439 while validation log loss spikes from 0.3124 to 0.7767. The best ModernBERT model (epoch 1) achieves 0.3124 val log loss, but XGBoost stacking degrades it to 0.5383, confirming that engineered features and stacking hurt performance. Historical successful experiments show that single-stage end-to-end fine-tuning with proper regularization (warmup, gradient accumulation, lower LR, label smoothing) consistently outperforms hybrid stacking. Historical failed experiments highlight that mixup causes instability and that increasing dropout or using layer-wise decay can be detrimental when not needed. Therefore, I focus on two modules: model_design (remove stacking, add label smoothing) and training_evaluation (replace two-stage with single-stage end-to-end, add gradient accumulation with warmup and cosine decay, reduce learning rate). Data processing remains unchanged since engineered features are no longer needed when using a single ModernBERT only.",
